{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And you shall keep, etc.</b> One needs to know why He repeated the very same things He said in the preceding verse. And it may be explained according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 74a), who said: ״וָחַי בָּהֶם״ — and not that he should die by them, meaning, if one is coerced to transgress one of the commandments of HaShem, he should transgress and not be killed. And they further said that for three transgressions a person is obligated to be killed rather than transgress, and they are: idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and bloodshed. Accordingly, the first verse was stated adjacent to the commandments of forbidden relations, and the verse did not stipulate ״אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה וְגוֹ׳ וָחַי בָּהֶם״. And a second verse was stated to command concerning the rest of the commandments, and it said <b>וָחַי בָּהֶם</b> to draw the inference and say that regarding the commandments stated in the preceding verse, where it did not say ״וָחַי בָּהֶם״ — there one should be killed rather than transgress." "<b>Further,</b> it appears fitting to raise another question: why did the verse change its language between the two verses — in the first it said ״אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״, and in the second verse it said ״אֲנִי ה׳״? Indeed, the Torah spoke corresponding to two types of children: one who acts from fear and one who acts from love. And the matter has already been explained in other places (Sotah 31a): that the one who acts from fear, his reward extends to a thousand generations, while the one who acts from love, his reward is doubled to two thousand. Now, it is impossible for one who attains the level of love to do so unless the level of fear precedes it, in the sod of (Psalms 118:20) ״זֶה הַשַּׁעַר לַה׳״ — ״This is the gate to HaShem.״ Therefore the verse, corresponding to the level of fear, said ״אֶת מִשְׁפָּטַי וְגוֹ׳ <b>אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם</b>״ — it was precise to mention the aspect of strict judgment, which is alluded to in the name Elohim, to alert you regarding who it is that the verse is speaking of. And afterwards it commanded a second measure: to act from love. And for this reason it said <b>אֲנִי ה׳</b> and did not mention the divine name Elohim, to say that the keeping should be on account of love of the good and the desire and the will that act upon him to fulfill the statement of his beloved Maker out of love for Him — even if there were no punishment for transgressing." "And it said <b>וָחַי בָּהֶם</b>, meaning that a person who acts from love also receives good in this world, beyond the reward fixed for him in the eternal world. And perhaps this is what it alluded to by saying ״וָחַי״ with the addition of the letter vav, to alert you that this is an addition upon another reward, which is the hidden reward; unlike the first measure — one who acts from fear — who has only the supernal world, as it says ״לָלֶכֶת בָּהֶם״ — in the supernal world. But this measure, ״וָחַי בָּהֶם״ — meaning, not for the future alone, for if so it should have said ״וְיִחְיֶה״, but rather from the moment he tastes the flavor of the commandments, in this respect: ״וָחַי בָּהֶם״. And according to this approach, one may also explain what it says in parashat (Deuteronomy 11:13–14) ״וְהָיָה אִם שָׁמֹעַ״, where it says ״וְנָתַתִּי מְטַר אַרְצְכֶם״ etc. — meaning, because there it says ״לְאַהֲבָה אֶת ה׳״ etc., therefore He informed us that this commandment has reward also in this world, and it is fitting to enjoy both worlds. And although the Sages of blessed memory (Kiddushin 39b) said that the reward for commandments in this world does not exist, this exists as a gratuitous kindness. And He alluded to the supernal world by what it says ״וְנָתַתִּי״ with the addition of the vav at the beginning of the promise of reward, to say: this is only an addition upon the hidden, intelligible, eternal reward." "<b>The human, etc.</b> In tractate Shabbat, and this is their language (Bava Kamma 38a; Avodah Zarah 3a): Rabbi Yehudah said, from where do we learn that even a gentile who engages in Torah is like the High Priest? For it is written ״אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה אוֹתָם הָאָדָם וָחַי״ etc. — priests, Levites, Israelites are not mentioned, but rather ״הָאָדָם״, end of quote. The meaning is that just as the superfluity of ״הָאָדָם״ posed a difficulty for Rabbi Yehudah, a further difficulty posed itself to him: why did the verse speak in covert language? For it should have said ״אֲשֶׁר תַּעֲשׂוּ אוֹתָם״ in the manner it began to speak — ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם״ etc. — in the second person. And from this force he derived that the verse intended one who was not present in that standing — who is neither priest nor Levite nor Israelite. And who is that? You would say: a gentile. And the Tosafot of blessed memory raised a difficulty from a baraita (Bava Metzia 114b) where Rabbi Shimon derived the verse (Ezekiel 34:31) ״אָדָם אַתֶּם״ — ״You are called adam״ etc. And they said that one must distinguish between ״אָדָם״ and ״הָאָדָם״, end of quote. And likewise I saw in the Book of the Zohar (vol. 1, 25a) that this distinction is intrinsically true, and it is a matter dependent on numerical calculation, as is known to those who know grace (ליודעי חן), for the numerically superior calculation is that of ״אָדָם״ and not of ״הָאָדָם״, for one who adds, subtracts (המוסיף גורע). Or perhaps, when a gentile engages in Torah, this crown too rests upon him to be called ״אָדָם״, and accordingly the letter heh in ״הָאָדָם״ becomes an inclusion of the gentile — that he too is called adam when he engages in Torah. And the letter heh here is not part of the essence of the name. And understand this."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:30.901243555Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}